body:
Yep, that is my understanding as well. I modified my test script to pull the `mix_rate` from `AudioServer`, like so:
```gdscript
extends AudioStreamPlayer2D

@onready var playback: AudioStreamGeneratorPlayback
var capture_effect: AudioEffectCapture

func _ready():
	play()

        # Set mix rate to what the AudioServer is outputting
	(stream as AudioStreamGenerator).mix_rate = AudioServer.get_mix_rate()
	playback = get_stream_playback()
	var bus_index = AudioServer.get_bus_index("Capturer")
	var num_effects = AudioServer.get_bus_effect_count(bus_index)
	capture_effect = AudioServer.get_bus_effect(bus_index, num_effects - 1)

func _process(delta):
	var frames_available = capture_effect.get_frames_available()
	if playback.can_push_buffer(frames_available):
		var buf = capture_effect.get_buffer(frames_available)
		capture_effect.clear_buffer()
		playback.push_buffer(buf)
```
And with this, it works! 🥳 

What I would consider the bug behavior is that, as far as I can see in the project settings, there's no indication that the set "Mix Rate" is being overridden with a different value.

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/296a40d6-736f-4eca-8cec-f6d626288c50)

Renaming this setting to something like "Target Mix Rate" could help with the confusion, but I imagine that'd also be a breaking change (I think the names in Project Settings are generated from the internal property names?)

Based on what I know of the Project Settings UI, this may be hard to do, but it could be nice for it to detect if the user is on macOS/iOS/iPadOS (or any platform affected by #88628), or if the project export settings target at least one of those platforms, and if so display an extra little warning message that the mix rate may be different on those platforms.

I definitely like the idea of `AudioStreamGenerator` having presets to just use whatever the `AudioServer`'s input/output rate is.
